<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="glycine degradation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: TAX-562| is able to grow with |FRAME: GLY| as the sole nitrogen source |CITS: [4257200]|. As |FRAME: TAX-562| lacks a glycine transaminase, glycine needs to be converted to |FRAME: SER|. This is achieved by the combined activities of |FRAME: EC-1.4.1.27| (GCV), cleaving one |FRAME: GLY| molecule to |FRAME: AMMONIUM|, |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE|, and |FRAME: METHYLENE-THF-GLU-N 5,10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate|, and |FRAME: EC-2.1.2.1|, which subsequently condenses the latter with a second |FRAME: GLY| molecule to form |FRAME: SER|. This pathway has been shown to be used by &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; when utilizing glycine as the sole source of nitrogen |CITS: [764875]|. Once formed, |FRAME: SER| can liberate ammonium via |FRAME: EC-4.3.1.17| |CITS: [764875]|, or it can serve as an amine group donor via the activity of aminotransferases |CITS: [35876664]|. The pathway is common, seen in many organisms. It is used as the main carbon pathway by several microorganisms, including |FRAME: TAX-33033| growing with glycine |CITS: [14456783]|, and various methylotrophic bacteria growing on one-carbon compounds |CITS: [13651189][4929654]|. It is also a part of the photorespiration pathway found in higher plants (see |FRAME: PWY-181|).</Description>
